<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  ============================================================================
  THE FILE TYPES AQUARIUS INSTALLER OPENS THAT LINUX HAS NO NAME FOR
  ============================================================================
  PLAIN ENGLISH

  Linux works out what a file is from a shared list of file types that ships
  with the system. Double-clicking a file only does something if that list has
  a name for it, so anything the list has never heard of is "unknown", and an
  unknown file cannot be offered an app to open it with.

  The shared list already knows most of what a person downloads: .rpm, .deb,
  .zip, .tar.gz, .AppImage, .flatpakref, .exe, .msi, .dmg. This file covers the
  four endings where it does not, or where the name it gives is no use for
  deciding what opens the file:

    *.run     a vendor's own installer program. The shared list has no name
              for it at all, so it is "unknown" without this.
    *.snap    an app in the Snap format, which AquariusOS does not use. Newer
              copies of the shared list do know this one; the entry here means
              the type exists whether they do or not.
    *.pkg     a Mac installer. The shared list calls it an "xar archive",
              which is true and useless: it says what the container is, not
              that double-clicking it should explain why it cannot run here.
    *.AppImage  known, but see the note further down about capitalisation.

  ⚠️ WHY REGISTER TYPES WE REFUSE TO INSTALL. Because the alternative is worse.
  Double-clicking a .snap or a .pkg on a computer that has never heard of them
  does NOTHING AT ALL — no window, no message, no clue. With this file, the
  same double-click opens one window that says, in one sentence, why that file
  cannot run here and what to look for instead. An honest answer beats silence.

  WHERE THE NAMES COME FROM. There are no official ones, so these follow the
  ordinary convention for a type nobody has registered: `application/x-` and
  then a word. If the freedesktop project ever publishes real ones, this file
  is where they change, and they change in exactly one place.

  WHY THE GLOBS HAVE A WEIGHT. A `.run` file is also, technically, a shell
  script, and the shared list may well already say so. Weight 60 beats the
  default 50, so a file called `installer.run` is an installer program here
  rather than a text file to edit.

  ⚠️ `.sh` IS DELIBERATELY NOT CLAIMED. A shell script is a text file people
  write and edit, and making the installer the thing that opens every one of
  them would be a much bigger nuisance than the one it fixes. A `.sh` dropped
  ON the window is still sorted properly; it just is not what double-clicking
  one does.

  HOW IT REACHES THE SYSTEM. The image build copies this into
  /usr/share/mime/packages/ and then runs `update-mime-database /usr/share/mime`,
  which folds every file in that folder into the one list the whole desktop
  reads. build_files/65-installer.sh reads the result back out.
  ============================================================================
-->
<mime-info xmlns="http://www.freedesktop.org/standards/shared-mime-info">

  <mime-type type="application/x-aquarius-run-installer">
    <comment>Installer program</comment>
    <glob pattern="*.run" weight="60"/>
    <generic-icon name="application-x-executable"/>
  </mime-type>

  <mime-type type="application/vnd.snap">
    <comment>Snap package</comment>
    <glob pattern="*.snap" weight="60"/>
    <generic-icon name="package-x-generic"/>
  </mime-type>

  <mime-type type="application/x-apple-installer-package">
    <comment>Mac installer package</comment>
    <glob pattern="*.pkg" weight="60"/>
    <generic-icon name="package-x-generic"/>
  </mime-type>

  <!--
    And one belt-and-braces glob for AppImages. The shared list does know this
    type, and it recognises the file by its contents as well as its name — but
    an AppImage arrives from a web browser under every capitalisation there is,
    and a file whose type is decided by its name alone must not depend on which.
  -->
  <mime-type type="application/vnd.appimage">
    <comment>AppImage application</comment>
    <glob pattern="*.AppImage" weight="60"/>
    <glob pattern="*.appimage" weight="60"/>
    <generic-icon name="application-x-executable"/>
  </mime-type>

</mime-info>
